── Section 1: Tensor Core Throughput — B200 vs H100 ──

  Blackwell's 5th-generation Tensor Cores add FP4 and approximately
  double throughput at every precision level relative to Hopper (H100).

  FP4 format: 3-bit mantissa, 0-bit exponent, per-block scale factor.
  Weights stored in FP4, dequantized on-chip before accumulation.
  Accuracy: < 1% perplexity degradation vs FP16 for most LLMs.

  Precision        B200 Peak     H100 Peak   Ratio  Use Case
  ────────────  ────────────  ────────────  ──────  ────────────────────────────────
  FP4            ~20 PFLOP/s           N/A     NEW  Weight-only LLM inference
  FP8            ~10 PFLOP/s  ~3.9 PFLOP/s    2.6×  LLM training & inference
  FP16/BF16       ~5 PFLOP/s  ~1.98 PFLOP/s    2.5×  Mixed-precision training
  TF32          ~2.5 PFLOP/s  ~989 TFLOP/s    2.5×  Default FP32 matmul
  INT8              ~20 TOPS     ~7.9 TOPS    2.5×  Post-training quantization

  B200 FP16/BF16 Tensor Core speedup over H100: 2.53×
  ✓ Section 1 passed

── Section 2: Roofline Ridge Points Across Generations ──

  The ridge point = peak FLOP/s / memory bandwidth.
  It is the arithmetic intensity (FLOPs/byte) above which a kernel is
  compute-bound; below which it is memory-bandwidth-bound.

  Blackwell's proportional improvement in both FLOP/s and bandwidth
  keeps the ridge point nearly constant vs Hopper — the SAME kernels
  that are memory-bound on H100 remain memory-bound on B200.
  The benefit for memory-bound workloads is raw bandwidth (8 vs 3.35 TB/s).

  GPU                 FP16 TFLOP/s   HBM BW GB/s   Ridge (FLOP/byte)
  ────────────────  ──────────────  ────────────  ──────────────────
  A100 SXM4                    312         2,000               156.0
  H100 SXM5                  1,979         3,350               590.7
  B200 SXM                   5,000         8,000               625.0
  RTX 5090                     838         1,792               467.6
  RTX 4060                     136           272               500.0
  MI300X                     1,307         5,300               246.6

  H100 ridge: 590.7  B200 ridge: 625.0  Difference: 5.8%
  → Same workloads remain memory-bound on B200 as on H100.
  ✓ Section 2 passed

── Section 3: Model Fit on B200 (192 GB HBM3e) ──

  Memory required for LLM inference ≈ params × bytes_per_param.
  B200 has 192 GB HBM3e; H100 has 80 GB HBM3.

  FP16: 2 bytes/param   FP8: 1 byte/param   FP4: 0.5 bytes/param

  Model             Precision   GB Required    Fits B200?    H100s needed
  ────────────────  ────────  ────────────  ────────────  ──────────────
  Llama 3  8B       FP16                16         Yes ✓               1
  Llama 3  8B       FP8                  8         Yes ✓               1
  Llama 3  8B       FP4                  4         Yes ✓               1
  Llama 3 70B       FP16               140         Yes ✓               2
  Llama 3 70B       FP8                 70         Yes ✓               1
  Llama 3 70B       FP4                 35         Yes ✓               1
  Llama 3 405B      FP16               810         No  ✗              11
  Llama 3 405B      FP8                405         No  ✗               6
  Llama 3 405B      FP4                202         No  ✗               3

  Llama 3 70B FP16: 140 GB — fits B200 ✓, needs 2× H100 ✓
  ✓ Section 3 passed

── Section 4: AllReduce Timing — A100 vs H100 vs B200 Cluster ──

  Ring AllReduce formula (Chapter 13):
    t = 2 × (N-1)/N × tensor_bytes / link_bw_bytes_per_sec

  NVLink generations:
    NVLink 3 (A100): 600 GB/s per GPU pair
    NVLink 4 (H100): 900 GB/s per GPU pair
    NVLink 5 (B200): 1,800 GB/s per GPU pair

  AllReduce of 1 GB tensor, 8 GPUs:

  Cluster                    NVLink BW    AllReduce (ms)
  ──────────────────────  ────────────  ────────────────
  A100 (NVLink 3)                600 GB/s            2.92 ms
  H100 (NVLink 4)                900 GB/s            1.94 ms
  B200 (NVLink 5)               1800 GB/s            0.97 ms

  B200 AllReduce is 2.0× faster than H100 for 8-GPU ring.
  ✓ Section 4 passed

── Section 5: GB200 Grace Blackwell — Eliminating the PCIe Bottleneck ──

  The GB200 Superchip connects a Grace CPU to a Blackwell GPU via
  NVLink-C2C at 900 GB/s — 28× faster than PCIe 4.0 x16 (~32 GB/s).

  DataLoader implications (Chapter 7 revisited):
    PCIe systems  → pin_memory + non_blocking are CRITICAL.
    GB200 systems → H2D is no longer the bottleneck; profiling target
                    shifts to CPU preprocessing (py-spy, not idle_pct).

  Transfer Type                     PCIe 4.0    NVLink-C2C   Speedup
  ────────────────────────────  ────────────  ────────────  ────────
  CPU DRAM → GPU VRAM                   32 GB/s         900 GB/s      28×
  GPU VRAM → CPU DRAM                   32 GB/s         900 GB/s      28×
  Effective DataLoader BW               12 GB/s         400 GB/s      33×

  Key architectural shift:
    PCIe (most GPUs): DataLoader bottleneck → pin_memory, prefetch
    NVLink-C2C (GB200): CPU preprocessing bottleneck → py-spy, codec

  ✓ Section 5 passed — Grace Blackwell eliminates PCIe H2D bottleneck

